window.CalcPast["2021-1"] = {"year":"2021–2022","sitting":1,"kind":"Exam","date":"June 23, 2022","iso":"2022-06-23","minutes":120,"file":"calc-exam-2021-01","instructions":["You get 10 points for writing your name and student number on each page (the grade is (points + 10)/10).","For each item the maximum score is indicated; in total you can score 100 points.","A regular calculator is allowed; graphical/programmable calculators, phones and other devices are not.","You have 120 minutes (most students with extra time: 150 minutes).","Always explain your calculations. Do not write numbers in digits (e.g. 0.25) but use fractions (e.g. 1/4) and simplify as much as possible."],"questions":[{"no":"1","points":9,"intro":"","items":[{"label":"","prompt":"If $$\\lim_{x\\to1}\\frac{A\\pi\\tan(\\pi x)+B\\pi^2\\ln(x)+C\\cos(\\pi x)+3}{\\pi^2(x-1)^2}=2$$ then what are the values of $A$, $B$, and $C$? [Hint: $A, B, C\\in\\mathbb{Z}$]","parts":[{"kind":"number","label":"$A =$","answer":"1","latex":"1"},{"kind":"number","label":"$B =$","answer":"-1","latex":"-1"},{"kind":"number","label":"$C =$","answer":"3","latex":"3"}],"points":9,"topic":"lhopital","hints":["The denominator tends to $0$, so the numerator must tend to $0$ as well — that gives a first equation. Then apply L'Hôpital and repeat the argument; the last step gives the value $2$.","$A =$ The answer is a positive integer. $B =$ The answer is a negative integer. $C =$ The answer is a positive integer."],"solution":["Denominator $\\to 0$, so the numerator must $\\to 0$: $C\\cos\\pi + 3 = 0 \\Rightarrow C = 3$.","L'Hôpital: $\\dfrac{\\frac{A\\pi^2}{\\cos^2(\\pi x)} + \\frac{B\\pi^2}{x} - C\\pi\\sin(\\pi x)}{2\\pi^2(x-1)}$. The numerator must vanish at $x=1$: $A\\pi^2 + B\\pi^2 = 0 \\Rightarrow B = -A$.","L'Hôpital again: $\\dfrac{\\frac{2A\\pi^3\\sin(\\pi x)}{\\cos^3(\\pi x)} - \\frac{B\\pi^2}{x^2} - C\\pi^2\\cos(\\pi x)}{2\\pi^2} \\to \\dfrac{-B\\pi^2 + C\\pi^2}{2\\pi^2} = \\dfrac{C - B}{2}$.","$\\dfrac{3 - B}{2} = 2 \\Rightarrow B = -1$, so $A = 1$, $B = -1$, $C = 3$."]}]},{"no":"2","points":10,"intro":"","items":[{"label":"","prompt":"If $$\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{r=1}^{n}\\frac{9r^2+4rn+5n^2}{(n+r)(n^2+r^2)} = A\\ln(2)$$ then what is the value of $A$? [Hint: $A\\in\\mathbb{N}$]","parts":[{"kind":"number","label":"$A =$","answer":"7","latex":"7"}],"points":10,"topic":"riemann","hints":["Divide numerator and denominator by $n^3$ to write the sum as $\\frac1n\\sum f(\\frac rn)$; the limit is $\\int_0^1 f(x)\\,dx$. Then use partial fractions.","The answer is a positive integer."],"solution":["$\\displaystyle\\lim\\frac1n\\sum_{r=1}^n\\frac{9(\\frac rn)^2 + 4\\frac rn + 5}{(1+\\frac rn)(1+(\\frac rn)^2)} = \\int_0^1\\frac{9x^2+4x+5}{(1+x)(1+x^2)}dx$.","Partial fractions: $\\dfrac{5}{1+x} + \\dfrac{4x}{1+x^2}$.","$= 5\\ln|1+x|\\Big|_0^1 + 2\\ln(1+x^2)\\Big|_0^1 = 5\\ln 2 + 2\\ln 2 = 7\\ln 2$ (the second term with $u = 1 + x^2$).","So $A = 7$."]}]},{"no":"3","points":15,"intro":"Select the best fitting answer.","items":[{"label":"(a)","prompt":"Let $f(x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-x}}{x^2-x-6}$. Then the domain of $f$ is","parts":[{"kind":"choice","label":"","options":["$(-\\infty,1)$","$(-\\infty,-2)\\cup(-2,1)$","$(-\\infty,1)\\setminus\\{-2,3\\}$","None of the above"],"answer":3,"latex":"None of the above"}],"points":3,"topic":"domain-range","hints":["Root: $1 - x \\ge 0$. Denominator: $x^2 - x - 6 = (x-3)(x+2) \\neq 0$. Is $x = 1$ allowed?"],"solution":["$1 - x \\ge 0 \\iff x \\le 1$, and $x \\neq -2, 3$.","So $D_f = (-\\infty,-2)\\cup(-2,1]$. Since $1$ belongs to the domain, none of (A)–(C) is right: **(D)**."]},{"label":"(b)","prompt":"Let $f(x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-x}}{x^2-x-6}$. How many asymptotes (both horizontal and vertical) does $f$ have?","parts":[{"kind":"choice","label":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"answer":1,"latex":"2"}],"points":3,"topic":"asymptotes","hints":["Only the part $x \\le 1$ is in the domain. Which zeros of the denominator lie there, and what happens as $x\\to-\\infty$?"],"solution":["Vertical asymptote at $x = -2$ ($x = 3$ is outside the domain).","As $x\\to-\\infty$: $f(x)\\to0$, so $y = 0$ is a horizontal asymptote. Total: **2 (B)**."]},{"label":"(c)","prompt":"Let $f(x)$ be an odd function, and $g(x)$ be an even function, then","parts":[{"kind":"choice","label":"","options":["$(f\\circ g)(x)$ is even, and $(g\\circ f)(x)$ is even.","$(f\\circ g)(x)$ is odd, and $(g\\circ f)(x)$ is even.","$(f\\circ g)(x)$ is even, and $(g\\circ f)(x)$ is odd.","$(f\\circ g)(x)$ is odd, and $(g\\circ f)(x)$ is odd."],"answer":0,"latex":"$(f\\circ g)(x)$ is even, and $(g\\circ f)(x)$ is even."}],"points":3,"topic":"even-odd","hints":["Write out $f(g(-x))$ and $g(f(-x))$ and use the parity of the inner function first."],"solution":["$f(g(-x)) = f(g(x))$: even.","$g(f(-x)) = g(-f(x)) = g(f(x))$: even. **(A)**"]},{"label":"(d)","prompt":"Given is the following graph of the functions $f$, $f^{\\prime}$, and $f^{\\prime\\prime}$. [[img:assets/past-exams/2021-06-q3d-graph.png|Graph with a blue, a red and a green curve on [-3, 3]]] Identify which curve belongs to $f$, $f^{\\prime}$, and $f^{\\prime\\prime}$.","parts":[{"kind":"choice","label":"","options":["$f$ = blue, $f'$ = red, $f''$ = green","$f$ = blue, $f'$ = green, $f''$ = red","$f$ = red, $f'$ = blue, $f''$ = green","$f$ = red, $f'$ = green, $f''$ = blue","$f$ = green, $f'$ = blue, $f''$ = red","$f$ = green, $f'$ = red, $f''$ = blue"],"answer":1,"latex":"$f$ = blue, $f'$ = green, $f''$ = red"}],"points":3,"topic":"function-analysis","hints":["Where a curve has a horizontal tangent (minimum/maximum), its derivative must cross or touch $0$."],"solution":["Blue has its minimum at $x=0$; green is $0$ at $x=0$, negative left and positive right: green $= $ blue$^{\\prime}$.","Green increases on $(-2,2)$ and decreases outside; red is positive on $(-2,2)$ and negative outside: red $=$ green$^{\\prime}$. **(B)**"]},{"label":"(e)","prompt":"Use the same graph as in (d). Identify where the blue curve is concave.","parts":[{"kind":"choice","label":"","options":["$(0,\\infty)$","$(-2,2)$","$(-2,0)\\cup(0,2)$","$(-\\infty,-2)\\cup(2,\\infty)$"],"answer":3,"latex":"$(-\\infty,-2)\\cup(2,\\infty)$"}],"points":3,"topic":"concavity","hints":["Blue is $f$; concave means $f'' < 0$ — read off where the red curve is negative."],"solution":["$f'' = $ red $< 0$ on $(-\\infty,-2)\\cup(2,\\infty)$. **(D)**"]}]},{"no":"4","points":5,"intro":"","items":[{"label":"","prompt":"For what value of $a\\in\\mathbb{N}$ is the following function a probability distribution: $$f(x)=\\begin{cases}\\dfrac{a^2+2a}{(a+2\\sqrt{x})^2\\sqrt{x}} & \\text{if } 1\\le x\\le 4\\\\ 0 & \\text{elsewhere}\\end{cases}$$","parts":[{"kind":"number","label":"$a =$","answer":"4","latex":"4"}],"points":5,"topic":"continuous","hints":["Set $\\int_1^4 f(x)\\,dx = 1$ and substitute $u = a + 2\\sqrt x$, $du = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt x}dx$.","The answer is a positive integer."],"solution":["$u = a + 2\\sqrt x$, $du = \\frac{dx}{\\sqrt x}$, bounds $a+2$ to $a+4$.","$\\displaystyle 1 = \\int_{a+2}^{a+4}\\frac{a(a+2)}{u^2}du = -a(a+2)\\Big[\\frac1u\\Big]_{a+2}^{a+4} = \\frac{2a}{a+4}$.","$2a = a + 4 \\Rightarrow a = 4$."]}]},{"no":"5","points":5,"intro":"","items":[{"label":"","prompt":"A wooden figure has the shape of a near-cone by rotating the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{5}\\sqrt[3]{x}$ around the $x$-axis. It is 8 cm high. We can compute the volume over $[a,b]$ for any function $g(x)$ as the integral $$\\int_a^b\\pi(g(x))^2\\,dx,$$ i.e., for the wooden figure $a=0$, $b=8$, $g(x)=f(x)$. We want to create a similar figure, but the shape is too difficult to replicate. We decide to make it a regular cone, that is by rotating $f(x)=ax$ around the $x$-axis. Since we want the figure to have the same height, and the same volume, what is the $a>0$ we should choose? [Hint: $a$ should be a rational number, enter it in the form $p/q$.]","parts":[{"kind":"number","label":"$a =$","answer":"3/4","latex":"\\frac{3}{4}"}],"points":5,"topic":"area","hints":["Compute both volumes with $\\pi\\int_0^8 g(x)^2dx$ and set them equal.","The answer is a positive fraction (not an integer)."],"solution":["$\\pi\\int_0^8 5x^{2/3}dx = \\pi\\big[3x^{5/3}\\big]_0^8 = 96\\pi$.","$\\pi\\int_0^8 a^2x^2dx = a^2\\pi\\frac{512}{3}$.","$a^2 = 96\\cdot\\frac{3}{512} = \\frac{9}{16}$, so $a = \\frac34$."]}]},{"no":"6","points":5,"intro":"","items":[{"label":"","prompt":"A $n$-sided dice has $n$ sides with pips $1, 2, \\dots, n$. What is the expectation of a 41-sided dice?","parts":[{"kind":"number","label":"$E(X) =$","answer":"21","latex":"21"}],"points":5,"topic":"discrete","hints":["Each side has probability $\\frac1n$; use $\\sum_{i=1}^n i = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}$.","The answer is a positive integer."],"solution":["$E(X) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\frac1n\\, i = \\frac1n\\cdot\\frac{n(n+1)}2 = \\frac{n+1}{2}$.","For $n = 41$: $E(X) = 21$."]}]},{"no":"7","points":10,"intro":"Answer T(rue) or F(alse).","items":[{"label":"(a)","prompt":"$f(x)=\\dfrac{x^2-x-6}{x+2}$ has a slant asymptote as the degree in the numerator is one more than the degree of the denominator.","parts":[{"kind":"choice","label":"","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"latex":"False"}],"points":2,"topic":"asymptotes","hints":["Factor the numerator first."],"solution":["$\\frac{(x+2)(x-3)}{x+2} = x - 3$ with a hole at $x=-2$: the graph *is* a line, so there is no slant asymptote. **False**"]},{"label":"(b)","prompt":"If $f$ has an inflection point in $x$ then $f''(x)=0$ (assuming $f''$ exists).","parts":[{"kind":"choice","label":"","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"latex":"True"}],"points":2,"topic":"concavity","hints":["At an inflection point $f''$ changes sign."],"solution":["$f''$ changes sign there, so (when it exists) it equals $0$. **True**"]},{"label":"(c)","prompt":"$\\dfrac{2x(x+1)}{(x^2+1)^2}$ can be split into $\\dfrac{Ax+B}{(x^2+1)}+\\dfrac{Cx+D}{(x^2+1)^2}$.","parts":[{"kind":"choice","label":"","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"latex":"True"}],"points":2,"topic":"partial-fractions","hints":["What is the partial-fraction form for a repeated irreducible quadratic factor?"],"solution":["A repeated irreducible quadratic needs linear numerators over $(x^2+1)$ and $(x^2+1)^2$. **True**"]},{"label":"(d)","prompt":"The area between any two functions $f$ and $g$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by $\\int_a^b f(x)-g(x)\\,dx$ (assuming that both $f$ and $g$ are defined on $[a,b]$ and finite).","parts":[{"kind":"choice","label":"","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"latex":"False"}],"points":2,"topic":"area","hints":["What if $g$ lies above $f$ somewhere?"],"solution":["The area is $\\int_a^b|f(x)-g(x)|\\,dx$; without the absolute value parts can cancel. **False**"]},{"label":"(e)","prompt":"$\\lim_{x\\to a}[f(x)+g(x)]=\\lim_{x\\to a}f(x)+\\lim_{x\\to a}g(x)$ always holds.","parts":[{"kind":"choice","label":"","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"latex":"False"}],"points":2,"topic":"limits","hints":["The sum rule needs both limits to exist."],"solution":["Counterexample: $f(x) = \\frac1x$, $g(x) = -\\frac1x$ at $a = 0$: the left side is $0$, the right side does not exist. **False**"]}]},{"no":"8","points":5,"intro":"","items":[{"label":"","prompt":"Let $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{x^y}{e}$. First, determine $\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}f(x,y)$ and then use this result to determine the value $\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}f(e,2)\\in\\mathbb{N}$.","parts":[{"kind":"expr","label":"$\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}f(x,y) =$","answer":"x^y*(y*log(x) + 1)*exp(-1)/x","latex":"\\frac{x^{y} \\left(y \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1\\right)}{e x}","vars":["x","y"],"sample":{"x":[0.5,3.0],"y":[0.5,3.0]}},{"kind":"number","label":"Value at $(e,2)$:","answer":"3","latex":"3"}],"points":5,"topic":"partial-derivatives","hints":["Write $x^y = e^{y\\ln x}$: then $\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}x^y = x^y\\ln x$. Differentiate that with respect to $x$ (product rule).","$\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}f(x,y) =$ The answer contains $e$ and a logarithm. Value at $(e,2)$: The answer is a positive integer."],"solution":["$\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\frac{x^y}{e} = \\frac1e\\ln(x)\\,x^y$.","$\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\Big(\\frac1e\\ln(x)x^y\\Big) = \\frac1e\\Big(\\frac1x x^y + \\ln(x)\\,y\\,x^{y-1}\\Big) = \\frac1e(y\\ln x + 1)x^{y-1}$.","At $(e,2)$: $\\frac1e(2 + 1)e = 3$."]}]},{"no":"9","points":5,"intro":"","items":[{"label":"","prompt":"Given the function $f(x)=x^2\\sqrt{x^3-2}$. Let $F$ be the primitive of $f$ going through the point $P=(3,28)$. What is the $y$-intercept of the tangent line of $F$ going through $P$?","parts":[{"kind":"number","label":"$b =$","answer":"-107","latex":"-107"}],"points":5,"topic":"substitution","hints":["You do not need $F$ itself: the slope of the tangent is $F^{\\prime}(3) = f(3)$.","The answer is a negative integer."],"solution":["Slope $F^{\\prime}(3) = f(3) = 9\\cdot\\sqrt{25} = 45$.","$28 = 45\\cdot3 + b \\Rightarrow b = -107$."]}]},{"no":"10","points":10,"intro":"","items":[{"label":"","prompt":"Let $p(u)$, $q(u)$, and $r(u)$ be polynomials in $u$. We have $$\\int e^{3x}\\sin(e^x)\\,dx=\\int p(u)\\sin(u)\\,du=q(u)\\sin(u)+r(u)\\cos(u)+C$$ then what are $p(u)$, $q(u)$, and $r(u)$? (Use substitution to obtain $p(u)$, integration by parts to obtain $q(u)$ and $r(u)$. Give the polynomials in terms of the variable $u$, not in the original variable $x$.) Finally, use this to compute the value of the following definite integral. $$\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\ln(\\pi)}e^{3x}\\sin(e^x)\\,dx.$$","parts":[{"kind":"expr","label":"$p(u) =$","answer":"u^2","latex":"u^{2}","vars":["u"],"sample":{"u":[-2.0,2.0]}},{"kind":"expr","label":"$q(u) =$","answer":"2*u","latex":"2 u","vars":["u"],"sample":{"u":[-2.0,2.0]}},{"kind":"expr","label":"$r(u) =$","answer":"2 - u^2","latex":"2 - u^{2}","vars":["u"],"sample":{"u":[-2.0,2.0]}},{"kind":"number","label":"Value of the definite integral:","answer":"-4 + pi^2","latex":"-4 + \\pi^{2}"}],"points":10,"topic":"by-parts","hints":["Substitute $u = e^x$ ($du = e^x dx$, so $e^{3x}dx = u^2du$). Then integrate $u^2\\sin u$ by parts twice; the bounds become $0$ and $\\pi$.","$p(u) =$ The answer is a polynomial of degree 2 in $u$. $q(u) =$ The answer is a polynomial of degree 1 in $u$. $r(u) =$ The answer is a polynomial of degree 2 in $u$. Value of the definite integral: The exact answer is positive and involves $\\pi$."],"solution":["$u = e^x$: $\\int e^{3x}\\sin(e^x)dx = \\int u^2\\sin u\\,du$, so $p(u) = u^2$.","$\\int u^2\\sin u\\,du = -u^2\\cos u + 2\\int u\\cos u\\,du = -u^2\\cos u + 2u\\sin u - 2\\int\\sin u\\,du = 2u\\sin u + (2-u^2)\\cos u + C$.","So $q(u) = 2u$, $r(u) = 2 - u^2$.","Bounds: $x\\to-\\infty \\Rightarrow u\\to0$; $x = \\ln\\pi \\Rightarrow u = \\pi$.","Value: $[2u\\sin u + (2-u^2)\\cos u]_0^\\pi = (\\pi^2 - 2) - 2 = \\pi^2 - 4$."]}]},{"no":"11","points":11,"intro":"Compute the following limits.","items":[{"label":"(a)","prompt":"$\\lim_{t\\to0}t^2\\ln(6t^2)$","parts":[{"kind":"number","label":"Limit:","answer":"0","latex":"0"}],"points":6,"topic":"lhopital","hints":["Form $0\\cdot(-\\infty)$: rewrite as $\\dfrac{\\ln(6t^2)}{1/t^2}$ and apply L'Hôpital.","The answer is an integer."],"solution":["$\\lim\\frac{\\ln(6t^2)}{t^{-2}} \\overset{LH}{=} \\lim\\frac{12t/(6t^2)}{-2t^{-3}} = \\lim\\frac{2/t}{-2/t^3} = \\lim(-t^2) = 0$."]},{"label":"(b)","prompt":"$\\lim_{t\\to4}\\dfrac{8t-8\\sqrt{3t+4}}{t-4}$","parts":[{"kind":"number","label":"Limit:","answer":"5","latex":"5"}],"points":5,"topic":"limits","hints":["Form $\\tfrac00$: multiply numerator and denominator by $t + \\sqrt{3t+4}$ (or use L'Hôpital).","The answer is a positive integer."],"solution":["$8\\cdot\\dfrac{t-\\sqrt{3t+4}}{t-4}\\cdot\\dfrac{t+\\sqrt{3t+4}}{t+\\sqrt{3t+4}} = 8\\cdot\\dfrac{t^2-3t-4}{(t-4)(t+\\sqrt{3t+4})} = 8\\cdot\\dfrac{t+1}{t+\\sqrt{3t+4}}$.","At $t = 4$: $8\\cdot\\frac58 = 5$."]}]}],"id":"2021-1"};
